WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.100
Picture the world's third richest person for a second, Sir J.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:08.200
Brin, he, uh, he recently packed up his entire life.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:09.400
We've moved out of California.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:09.900
Exactly.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:14.160
Officially moved his residency to Nevada, but right now, at this exact moment,

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:20.100
he is still pumping a massive chunk of a hundred million dollar war chest

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:25.080
into a political committee, literally called building a better California.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:27.600
I mean, the irony there is almost too perfect.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:31.160
You have a guy funding a massive political fight to shape the future of a state.

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:35.200
He just, you know, officially abandoned and he abandoned it specifically

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.040
because of a looming, incredibly aggressive wealth tax that he is now

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:40.640
trying to kill from across the state line.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:42.520
It is wild to watch unfold.

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:42.740
Yeah.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.760
And we need to look closely at this because for you listening, whether you

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:49.480
live in Los Angeles or Omaha or Brooklyn, this is really the ultimate test

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:53.120
case for how America is going to handle extreme wealth in the 21st century.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:54.280
It absolutely is.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:58.000
So let's lay out what's actually hitting the California ballot this November.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.360
It's known as the California Billionaire Tax Act.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:07.800
And the proposal is a one time, 5% tax on residents who are worth over

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:12.640
$1.1 billion provided they've lived in the States since January 1st, right?

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:13.720
Yes, exactly.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.800
And when you break down the raw numbers, I mean, the scale of what they are

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:18.800
trying to do is staggering.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:23.160
We're talking about targeting a very specific, highly concentrated pool of

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:26.960
people. It's just over 200 billionaires in the entire state.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:28.040
Just 200 people?

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:29.440
Yeah, just over 200.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:33.960
But the revenue projection from that tiny exclusive group is massive.

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:39.200
Supporters claim this one time hit will generate $100 billion over five years.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:39.600
Wow.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:42.680
And they've already earmarked exactly where that money goes, which is

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:44.760
it's central to the fight we're seeing right now.

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:49.880
90% is slated directly for health care, specifically to offset federal spending

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:54.520
cuts. And then the remaining 10% gets split between education and food

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:55.680
assistance programs.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:02:00.440
OK, let's unpack this because on the surface, a hundred billion dollars for

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:02.920
health care sounds fantastic to most voters.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:03.960
Sure.

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:08.400
But from a purely mechanical standpoint, you know, taxing a billionaire isn't

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.640
like taxing a regular paycheck.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:13.600
We aren't talking about Scrooge McDuck vaults of cash here.

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:16.800
A billionaire's net worth is usually tied up in equity.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:17.160
Right.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:19.200
It's in the stock of the companies they founded.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:20.040
Exactly.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:24.800
So if you suddenly demand 5% of their total net worth, they can't just write a check.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:26.160
They have to liquidate.

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:31.240
They have to sell shares, which means giving up voting power and control of their own

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:32.240
companies.

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:35.480
It sounds like trying to harvest golden eggs by threatening the goose.

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:37.400
I mean, is this actually sustainable?

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:40.880
Well, you're hitting on the exact friction point that has turned this into

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:43.160
such a massive nationwide political battle.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:47.360
They are taxing unrealized gains, not traditional income.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:47.680
Right.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:51.320
To put that in perspective for everyday life, imagine getting a tax bill from the

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:55.160
government based on like the Zillow's estimate of your house.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:57.000
You haven't actually sold the house.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.080
You haven't realized any actual cash from it.

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:02.240
But the state says, Hey, your house went up in theoretical value.

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:05.120
So you owe us 5% of that total value right now.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.720
That's a brutal position to pay that tax.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:11.680
I'd literally have to sell my kitchen just to cover the bill for how some

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.280
still living in precisely.

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:17.640
And that mechanical reality is what makes the politics of this so fascinating.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:21.800
What's fascinating here is if you look at the landscape of who supports and who

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:26.400
opposes this tax, the traditional partisan lines have completely blurred.

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:27.040
How so?

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:30.280
Well, they've turned into this bizarre set of political alliances.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:34.600
On the pro-tax side, you have exactly who you might expect championing a heavy

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:35.400
wealth tax.

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:39.400
You have left-wing figures like Senator Bernie Sanders, Silicon Valley

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:44.560
Representative Rokana and the billionaire climate activist Tom Steyer.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:44.800
Right.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:45.560
That tracks perfectly.

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:48.720
That is the expected coalition for progressive tax policy.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:49.040
Yeah.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:53.400
But the opposition is where this entire narrative gets turned completely upside

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:57.400
down because you'd expect the opposition to be just conservative business groups.

00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:58.600
But that's not what's happening.

00:03:58.840 --> 00:03:59.360
Not at all.

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:03.680
Leading the charge against this tax is California's own democratic governor,

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:04.600
Gavin Newsom.

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:06.240
Wait, Newsom is fighting it.

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:06.600
Yeah.

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:11.400
He actually tried and failed, I should add, to block this measure from even

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:13.400
making it onto the ballot in the first place.

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:17.960
He is terrified that a state level billionaire tax will act as an

00:04:17.960 --> 00:04:21.240
accelerant for an exodus of wealth and innovation.

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:26.360
So instead of a state tax, he actually took to sub-stack to propose a

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:29.880
national billionaire tax, which is, I mean, that's an interesting maneuver.

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:33.400
Proposing a national tax on sub-stack feels like a convenient way to say,

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:39.200
I support taxing billionaires in theory, just not in a way that specifically

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:40.680
drains my state's economy.

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:41.000
Right.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:44.400
Because let's be honest, the national wealth tax is highly unlikely to pass

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:45.680
under the current administration.

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:49.840
It's a classic political deflection, but Newsom is definitely not alone in

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:51.120
fighting the local measure.

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:54.200
And this is where the alliances get truly strange.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:54.480
Yeah.

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:57.520
This is the part that really stopped me in my tracks when looking through the

00:04:57.520 --> 00:04:58.200
source material.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:04:58.600
Yeah.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:02.640
You have Planned Parenthood's California chapter publicly coming out

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:04.160
against the Billionaire Tax Act.

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:04.560
Yes.

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:09.040
They explicitly called the measure short-sighted and stated it does not

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:13.960
offer a sustainable solution to funding cuts, which seems completely

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:14.840
counterintuitive.

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:15.560
Exactly.

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:20.680
Why would a massive health care organization oppose a tax where 90% of

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:23.840
the revenue is legally earmarked to go directly to health care?

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:24.200
Right.

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:26.880
It doesn't make sense until you look at the second order effects.

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:30.480
What Planned Parenthood, along with several major unions and

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:35.080
Democrat-aligned organizations is actually terrified of is the exodus.

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:37.880
They are worried about losing the goose that lays the regular eggs.

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:38.560
Exactly.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:42.640
They are worried that if you hit these 200 billionaires with a one-time

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:47.520
five percent tax on unrealized gains, they won't just sell their stock.

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:49.640
They'll just leave the state like Sergey Brind did.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:53.640
And if they leave, the state permanently loses their regular annual

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:54.680
income tax revenue.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:59.080
And California relies disproportionately on the top one percent of

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:01.960
earners to fund its entire annual budget.

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:05.960
So the unions fear this one-time cash grab will threaten the

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.000
everyday income tax revenue that sustains the state year after year and

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:14.440
actually make other tax hikes on higher earners much harder to pass down the line.

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:16.480
So you have this incredibly tense situation.

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:19.800
54% of likely voters support the tax.

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:23.880
The public is generally on board with making billionaires pay up.

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:24.320
Yeah.

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:28.000
But the establishment, even the progressive establishment is terrified of the

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:31.520
actual mechanics of implementing it, which brings me to a really crucial

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:32.000
question.

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:32.440
Okay.

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:36.600
If we're watching tech billionaires like Sergey Brind flea, and we're

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:40.920
watching the political establishment panic over keeping them around, how

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:44.560
secure is the tech wealth generating these billions in the first place?

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:48.560
That is the essential question underlying this entire debate because we

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:52.200
treat that tech wealth as like an immutable fact of gravity, right?

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:56.560
But the billionaires fleeing California aren't just running from a tax bill.

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:01.480
They are operating in a sector that is suddenly facing a very cold, very harsh

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:02.320
reality check.

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:07.160
We're seeing a broader cooling in the tech sector right now, specifically

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:10.600
around the absolute height machine of artificial intelligence.

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:10.840
Yeah.

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:14.640
The public market is suddenly showing severe skepticism.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:17.520
I want to look at the actual market data on this because it's alarming.

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:20.440
Both the S&P 500 and the NASDAQ ended the week down.

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:25.760
Investors are treating tech and specifically AI chips and infrastructure

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:27.280
with extreme caution.

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:28.400
Yeah, they really are.

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:30.720
And the most glaring example of this is Oracle.

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:32.720
They just suffered a 19% drop.

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:36.000
That is their worst single week since the dot com bust of 2001.

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:40.280
And it's strictly due to investor anxiety over their massive AI investments.

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:43.880
The 2001 dot com bus comparison is incredibly telling.

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:47.560
Back then you had companies with massive valuations based purely on the

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:49.120
theoretical promise of the internet.

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:49.480
Right.

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:50.920
Eyeballs, not profits.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:52.040
Exactly.

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:54.720
The actual revenue models hadn't caught up to the hype.

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:58.920
Today, investors are looking at the mechanics of AI and realizing this isn't

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:00.720
just software you can scale for free.

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:05.240
When we talk about AI, capital expenditures, capex, we're talking about real

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:09.360
physical infrastructure like data centers, massive server farms, the size of

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:13.480
football fields, cooling systems that draw enough water and power to sustain a

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:17.160
small city, billions of dollars spend on Nvidia chips.

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:20.880
Investors are looking at that colossal spend and finally asking, well, where

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:22.840
is the actual return on investment?

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:27.440
It's not just the legacy tech companies like Oracle feeling the heat, even the

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:31.280
absolute kingmakers of the AI boom are visibly sweating.

00:08:31.480 --> 00:08:32.600
We're talking about open AI.

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:37.760
Yes, open AI is reportedly mulling over delaying its IPO until 2027.

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:40.240
They had originally hired bankers to go public this year.

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:43.880
They were ready, but they're looking around at the current market, looking at

00:08:43.880 --> 00:08:47.840
the hesitation around AI monetization and pumping the brakes hard.

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:52.120
And a major piece of that hesitation seems to be a massive undeniable

00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:54.800
mismatch in valuation expectations.

00:08:55.200 --> 00:09:00.040
CEO Sam Altman wants the company to debut with a $1 trillion valuation.

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:00.600
Right.

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:06.440
But the last time they were officially valued, it came in at $730 billion.

00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:09.960
That is a $270 billion gap.

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:14.280
That's a quarter of a trillion dollars based purely on hype and expectation

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:15.880
that hasn't materialized yet.

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:18.960
And then that gap is terrifying when the market is turning cautious.

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:20.680
They're spooked by what they're seeing elsewhere.

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:24.080
Like they're watching SpaceX's share price slide in the private market.

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:24.680
Interesting.

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:25.160
Yeah.

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:29.680
And they know that a delayed IPO means their major rival and

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:33.440
thropic might actually beat them to the public market, which changes

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:34.960
the whole competitive landscape.

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:39.440
On top of that, open AI is now restricting initial access to its latest

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:43.080
AI models to a very small group of trusted partners.

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:43.520
Right.

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:46.280
And they're doing that specifically to assuage safety concerns from the

00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:49.520
US government, according to the reports, which means they can't just roll

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:52.080
this out to millions of paying users instantly.

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:52.600
Exactly.

00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:55.480
So the regulatory walls are closing in, slowing down their ability to generate

00:09:55.480 --> 00:09:58.800
revenue at the exact same time the financial optimism is cooling.

00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:02.960
So I have to push back here, or at least that's the really uncomfortable question.

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:03.560
Lay it on me.

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:07.160
Are we watching a massive bubble slowly deflate?

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:09.640
You mentioned the 2001.com bust.

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:13.040
Is the tech sector fundamentally sound right now?

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:17.040
Or have we just been writing a multi-year wave of irrational exuberance

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:20.120
about artificial intelligence and the bill is finally coming due?

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:21.320
It's a critical distinction.

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:25.040
It's not necessarily a complete deflation of the technology's value.

00:10:25.160 --> 00:10:29.000
I mean, AI will absolutely transform industries, but we're

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:31.920
undoubtedly watching the deflation of the timeline.

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:32.720
We'll get to time.

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:33.080
Right.

00:10:33.080 --> 00:10:35.920
We assumed AI would generate trillions of dollars tomorrow.

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:39.960
The market is now pricing in the reality that it might take a decade and cost a

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:42.000
fortune in energy and hardware to get there.

00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:42.480
Yeah.

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:47.040
And what makes this potential bubble deflating so dangerous is that it's not

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:48.080
happening in a vacuum.

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:51.560
The tech sector is heavily reliant on global supply chains for all those

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:52.400
ships and servers.

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:56.160
And right now, the traditional global markets and supply chains are trembling,

00:10:56.360 --> 00:10:58.520
which means we need to look at the physical world.

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:03.040
All that AI infrastructure relies on a global supply chain that is

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:04.920
currently under immense physical threat.

00:11:05.840 --> 00:11:09.600
Let's look at the Middle East because the geopolitical situation is directly

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:15.920
feeding into this global market anxiety and the cost of doing business everywhere.

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:17.120
Yeah, it really is.

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:18.320
Recently, U.S.

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:23.080
Central Command struck Iranian missile drone and coastal radar storage sites.

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:27.560
And the sequence of events leading up to that is critical to understand the

00:11:27.560 --> 00:11:28.880
fragility of the situation.

00:11:29.240 --> 00:11:33.520
It is if we connect this to the bigger picture, this military action didn't

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:34.800
just happen out of nowhere.

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:39.000
It came after former President Trump posted on Truth Social, stating that

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:41.040
Iran had violated a ceasefire agreement.

00:11:41.080 --> 00:11:41.360
Right.

00:11:41.680 --> 00:11:45.280
According to the post, Iran violated the deal by hitting a commercial cargo ship

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:47.200
in the Strait of Hormuz with a drone.

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:51.040
And for anyone who doesn't track global shipping routes, we have to

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:54.480
recognize why the Strait of Hormuz is so incredibly vital to everything we've

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:55.200
been talking about.

00:11:55.400 --> 00:11:59.320
It is arguably the world's most critical choke point for global shipping

00:11:59.320 --> 00:12:00.240
and oil transit.

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:05.160
Roughly a fifth of the world's daily oil consumption passes through that

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:06.280
narrow waterway.

00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:06.760
Wow.

00:12:06.760 --> 00:12:07.320
A fifth.

00:12:07.320 --> 00:12:07.760
Yeah.

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:12.440
When you have commercial cargo ships being struck by drones there and

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:16.880
retaliatory strikes by US Central Command on coastal radar sites, it

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:21.600
sends immediate, violent shockwaves through the global supply chain.

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:24.520
It creates massive volatility in energy markets.

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:27.120
It spikes the cost of shipping physical goods.

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:30.920
And this geopolitical instability is happening at the exact same time that

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:34.720
foundational, traditional industries, the ones that actually build physical

00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:37.880
things are experiencing their own internal crises.

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:38.240
Right.

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:41.800
The tech world is struggling with future promises and server costs.

00:12:42.200 --> 00:12:45.800
But the old world is struggling to survive today's energy prices and

00:12:45.800 --> 00:12:47.080
supply chain disruptions.

00:12:47.320 --> 00:12:49.400
Look at what's happening with legacy manufacturing.

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:50.480
Look at Volkswagen.

00:12:50.520 --> 00:12:54.120
Volkswagen is the perfect tragic example of this traditional industry

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:54.640
collapse.

00:12:54.680 --> 00:12:57.000
They're planning to slash 100,000 jobs.

00:12:57.400 --> 00:13:01.440
To put that in perspective, that is 15% of their entire global staff.

00:13:01.960 --> 00:13:06.080
And they're reportedly planning to stop manufacturing at four of their

00:13:06.080 --> 00:13:08.600
German facilities closing four plants in Germany.

00:13:09.560 --> 00:13:14.080
For Volkswagen, which is essentially a symbol of German industrial might,

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:16.920
that is historically unprecedented.

00:13:16.960 --> 00:13:17.960
It's monumental.

00:13:17.960 --> 00:13:20.200
It's an earthquake in European manufacturing.

00:13:20.240 --> 00:13:22.400
And the reason behind it is equally stark.

00:13:22.640 --> 00:13:27.560
They simply cannot compete with the wave of cheaper, highly advanced

00:13:27.560 --> 00:13:29.920
electric vehicles coming from Chinese brands.

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:30.160
Right.

00:13:30.160 --> 00:13:33.800
The EV market, Chinese manufacturers have optimized their supply chains and

00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:38.280
battery tech to a point where legacy automakers are bleeding cash trying to

00:13:38.280 --> 00:13:39.000
keep up.

